{"maxId":420,"entries":{"chapter-99-study-diabetes-mellitus|Chapter 99 Study: Diabetes Mellitus":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Diabetes: A1C ≥6.5%, FPG ≥126 mg/dL, or 2-h OGTT ≥200 mg/dL; any criterion should be confirmed on a separate day (Table 99-1 footnote, p1560); unless clear hyperglycemic symptoms, ADA verifies with two tests (p1561).":["u3"],"copy|Prediabetes: A1C 5.7–6.4%, FPG 100–125 mg/dL, or 2-h OGTT 140–199 mg/dL (p1560).":["u4"],"copy|~90% of older adults with diabetes have type 2 disease (p1560).":["u5"],"copy|GDM affects ~4% of US pregnancies; 5–10% of those women have persistent T2D postpartum; 20–50% develop T2D within 5–10 years (p1560).":["u6"],"copy|Screen all adults >65 with FPG and/or A1C; re-screen every 2–3 years; consider annual testing if high-risk (BMI≥25, first-degree relative, HTN, low HDL/high TG, Table 99-3) (p1561).":["u7"],"copy|Tests are often discordant; unless there are clear hyperglycemic symptoms, confirm with two diagnostic tests; if two tests are discordant, repeat the test whose result is above the diagnostic threshold (p1561).":["u8"],"copy|~27% of US adults 65+ have diabetes; ~50% have prediabetes (p1562).":["u9"],"copy|~20% of people meeting diabetes criteria are undiagnosed (p1561).":["u10"],"copy|New-case incidence peaks at 10/1000/yr (age 45–64) and 9/1000/yr (age 65–79), 2017–2018 (p1562).":["u11"],"copy|CVD/stroke history reported by 28% (35–64y), 43% (65–74y), 55% (75y+) of people with diabetes (p1562).":["u12"],"copy|Diabetic kidney disease (usually eGFR <60 and/or albuminuria >30 mg/g) present in ~50% of diabetics >65 (p1562); 5-year ESRD survival <40% (p1563).":["u13"],"copy|Retinopathy causes ~10,000 new US blindness cases/yr and may begin as early as 7 years before diagnosis in T2D (p1563).":["u14"],"copy|Neuropathy eventually develops in ~50% of diabetics (range 10–100% depending on criteria) (p1563).":["u15"],"copy|Lower-extremity amputation risk ~10-fold higher than in nondiabetic older adults (p1563).":["u16"],"copy|US diabetes cost >$300 billion (2017); 63% of inpatient diabetes costs are attributable to age 65+ (p1563).":["u17"],"copy|Depression: up to 60% higher incident-T2D risk per meta-analysis; diabetics have 2× the depression rate of nondiabetics (p1568).":["u18"],"copy|Normal aging causes only mild ↑ FPG but a more pronounced delay in glucose return-to-normal after an oral glucose challenge (p1563).":["u19"],"copy|Insulin resistance with aging tracks mainly with central adiposity and inactivity, not age per se (p1563–1564).":["u20"],"copy|β-cell secretory defects are demonstrable in older adults even when matched for insulin sensitivity (p1564).":["u21"],"copy|T2D genetics: ~70 susceptibility loci identified; ≥75% concordance in identical twins (p1565).":["u22"],"copy|DPP: intensive lifestyle intervention cut progression to T2D by >70% in patients >60 over 3 years of treatment, despite only 5–7% weight loss (p1565–1566).":["u23"],"copy|Metformin slowed progression in younger adults but had somewhat less effect in patients >60 (p1566).":["u24"],"copy|Benefit persisted at 15-year follow-up; diabetes onset delayed by ~4 years (p1566).":["u25"],"copy|Comprehensive geriatric assessment (Table 99-4): functional status (ADLs/IADLs), cognition, depression, falls, vision, frailty (p1567).":["u26"],"copy|Confirm albuminuria on 2 of 3 samples elevated over 3–6 months; old terms: microalbuminuria 30–300 mg/g, macroalbuminuria >300 mg/g (being retired) (p1567).":["u27"],"copy|Annual 10-g monofilament + 128-Hz tuning fork testing for neuropathy, starting at diagnosis (p1567).":["u28"],"copy|Cognitive screening at diagnosis, then annually if borderline or every 2–3 years if normal (p1568).":["u29"],"copy|3 tiers by comorbidity/function/cognition: Good, Intermediate, Poor health; Current practice (not the book): individualize targets (grade C): healthy older adults with few stable illnesses and intact cognition/function should have lower goals such as A1C <7.0-7.5% and/or CGM time in 70-180 mg/dL ≥70% and time ≤70 mg/dL ≤4%; complex/intermediate health prioritizes hypoglycemia avoidance, with less stringent goals such as A1C <8.0%, time in 70-180 mg/dL ≥50% and time <70 mg/dL <1%, considering cognition/function, frailty, comorbidity, medication risk-benefit and preferences; very complex/poor health avoids reliance on A1C and focuses on avoiding hypoglycemia and symptomatic hyperglycemia, with no numerical CGM goal specified; fasting/premeal and bedtime ranges respectively are 80-130/80-180 mg/dL (healthy), 90-150/100-180 (complex/intermediate) and 100-180/110-200 (very complex/poor); lower A1C goals require no recurrent/severe hypoglycemia or undue treatment burden - ADA Standards of Care 2026, Section 13, recommendations 13.7a-c pS282 and Table 13.2 pS284":["u30"],"copy|Good health: A1C <7.5% if not on a hypoglycemia-risk drug; ≥7.0 and <7.5% if on insulin/sulfonylurea/glinide.":["u31"],"copy|Intermediate health: <8% (not on risk drug) / ≥7.5 and <8.0% (on risk drug).":["u32"],"copy|Poor health: <8.5% (not on risk drug) / ≥8.0 and <8.5% (on risk drug).":["u33"],"copy|A1C–mortality relationship is U-shaped: lowest risk at 6–9% (p1574).":["u34"],"copy|BP target <140/90 mmHg for most; consider 130/80 mmHg for high-risk (prior stroke, progressing CKD), with orthostatic monitoring (p1572).":["u35"],"copy|ACE inhibitor or ARB first-line for hypertension when nephropathy is present (p1572).":["u36"],"copy|Statins may be indicated for nearly all older diabetics except those with end-stage disease and very limited life expectancy; benefit at age ≥75 without ASCVD is still being studied (p1571).":["u37"],"copy|If a statin alone doesn't reach the LDL-C goal, add ezetimibe or a PCSK9 inhibitor; if fasting triglycerides >500 mg/dL, add fish oil and/or fenofibrate to reduce pancreatitis risk (Table 99-8, p1572).":["u38"],"copy|Aspirin 75–162 mg/d recommended for secondary prevention (known CVD); primary prevention remains controversial because of bleeding risk (p1572).":["u39"],"copy|Annual eGFR + urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio screening (p1572).":["u41"],"copy|Annual comprehensive eye exam by an ophthalmologist (p1573).":["u42"],"copy|First-line agents for diabetic neuropathic pain: pregabalin, gabapentin, duloxetine (p1573).":["u43"],"copy|Metformin / metformin ER: printed dose lines 500–1000 mg BID, max 2500 mg/d, 500–2000 mg qd — the page does not pair each line to an agent (p1577). Cautions: avoid if eGFR <30; avoid in unstable heart failure or during acute hospitalization for major illness that could reduce tissue perfusion (lactic-acidosis risk) (p1576).":["u44"],"copy|Sulfonylureas (e.g., glipizide 2.5–10 mg qd–BID) (p1577). Cautions: hypoglycemia; avoid glyburide — long half-life, not recommended in older adults (p1579); avoid in renal/hepatic impairment (p1579); Required reading: see AGS Beers 2023 required-article card for qualified sulfonylurea avoidance and the preference if one is used (Table 2, p2061)":["u45"],"copy|DPP-4 inhibitors (e.g., sitagliptin 100 mg qd) (p1577). Cautions: renal dose-adjust (except linagliptin); possible pancreatitis; possible heart-failure hospitalization.":["u46"],"copy|GLP-1 agonists (e.g., liraglutide, semaglutide [also oral], dulaglutide) — injectable (semaglutide also oral), dose lines range from twice daily to once weekly; source table does not pair each dose line to its named agent (p1577–1578). Cautions: nausea/vomiting, possible pancreatitis, avoid exenatide/lixisenatide if eGFR <30.":["u47"],"copy|SGLT-2 inhibitors (e.g., empagliflozin 10–25 mg qd) (p1578). Cautions: avoid if eGFR <25–30 — Table 99-11 (p1578); genital/UTI infections; DKA (rare in T2DM; the volume effect may contribute) (p1578, p1580).":["u48"],"copy|Thiazolidinediones (pioglitazone 15–45 mg qd) (p1578). Cautions: avoid in heart failure (fluid retention), bone-fracture risk.":["u49"],"copy|Meglitinides (repaglinide 0.5–4 mg before meals) (p1578). Cautions: hypoglycemia; avoid combining with gemfibrozil (p1580).":["u50"],"copy|Glargine (basal): no peak, duration 20→>24 h; start 10 units qd or 0.2 units/kg/day.":["u51"],"copy|Degludec (ultra-long): duration >40 h.":["u52"],"copy|Rapid-acting: Table 99-12 prints onset 5–15 min, duration 2–4 h on the lispro line only (aspart/glulisine cells blank); rapid-acting analogs are given just before meals and can even be given within 20 min after starting a meal.":["u53"],"copy|Total daily dose: type 1 ~0.5–1.0 units/kg; type 2 up to 1.0–2.0 units/kg; start low, 0.1–0.2 units/kg/day.":["u54"],"copy|Titrate 2–5 units every 3–5 days (every 7 days for long-acting analogs).":["u55"],"copy|Diabetics may become symptomatic at 80–100 mg/dL, vs 50–60 mg/dL in nondiabetics — the counterregulatory threshold adapts to chronic glycemic exposure (p1583).":["u56"],"copy|Risk factors: insulin/sulfonylurea use, autonomic/glucagon impairment, renal/hepatic failure, cognitive disorder, sensory (vision/hearing) or functional (mobility/hand dexterity) impairment, poor/irregular nutrition, alcohol/sedative use, polypharmacy (Table 99-15) (p1584).":["u57"],"copy|β-blockers can interfere with hypoglycemia counterregulation — avoid combining with insulin/sulfonylurea when possible (p1584).":["u58"],"copy|Nursing-home diabetes prevalence: 26% men, 22% women (p1585).":["u59"],"copy|LTC residents show better observed survival at A1C 8–9% (p1585).":["u60"],"copy|End-of-life glucose target: 108–270 mg/dL (6–15 mmol/L) (p1586).":["u61"],"copy|The chapter states only insulin and sulfonylureas carry significant hypoglycemia risk, yet its own Table 99-6 and Table 99-11 also name glinides/meglitinides as hypoglycemia-risk drugs (p1576).":["u62"],"copy|Table 99-11 prints the DPP-4 row as \"↑ Glucagon secretion\", while the GLP-1 row directly below prints \"↓\" and the text says GLP-1 agonists suppress glucagon (p1580); the book does not reconcile this (p1577).":["u63"],"copy|Table 99-16 is sourced to the 2013 (2nd ed.) Diabetes UK end-of-life recommendations, while the chapter's own text notes they were last updated November 2021 (p1586).":["u64"],"recall|Recall":["u65"],"2026-exam-questions-for-this-chapter|2026 exam questions for this chapter":["u66"],"copy|א. High-intensity aerobic physical activity for at least 75 minutes a week, with no limit on consecutive rest days":["u67"],"copy|ב. High-intensity aerobic physical activity for at least 75 minutes a week, spread over at least 3 days, with at most two days without activity":["u68"],"copy|ג. Moderate-intensity aerobic physical activity for at least 150 minutes a week, with no limit on consecutive rest days":["u69"],"copy|ד. Moderate-intensity aerobic physical activity for at least 150 minutes a week, spread over at least 3 days, with at most two days without activity ← key":["u70"],"copy|א. Complete a periodic cognitive assessment every two to three years ← key":["u71"],"copy|ב. Start short-acting insulin around meals":["u72"],"copy|ג. Switch Insulin Toujeo to Insulin Lantus":["u73"],"copy|ד. Change the drug treatment in order to reach an HBA1C target of 8%":["u74"],"copy|א. Continue the current treatment ← key":["u75"],"copy|ב. Add long-acting insulin":["u76"],"copy|ג. Stop JARDIANCE":["u77"],"copy|ד. Switch to METFORMIN":["u78"],"copy|א. A fall in fasting glucose levels":["u79"],"copy|ב. A fall in blood insulin levels":["u80"],"copy|ג. An improvement in insulin sensitivity":["u81"],"copy|ד. Slower return to normal glucose levels after a sugar load ← key":["u82"],"copy|א. Stop the Metformin because of the eGFR values ← key":["u83"],"copy|ב. Add an SGLT-2 inhibitor as first line for treating her diabetic nephropathy":["u84"],"copy|ג. Replace the Metformin with a sulfonylurea of the Glyburide type":["u85"],"copy|ד. No need to change drug treatment, but electrolytes should be monitored more often":["u86"],"copy|CHAPTER 99s / HAZZARD":["u87"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 99 — Diabetes Mellitus; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u88"],"copy|Definition & diagnosis":["u89"],"copy|Epidemiology & complications":["u90"],"copy|Aging pathophysiology (brief)":["u91"],"copy|Prevention":["u92"],"copy|Evaluation":["u93"],"copy|Management goals — individualized A1C framework (Table 99-6, all p1571)":["u94"],"copy|CVD risk reduction":["u95"],"copy|Microvascular complications":["u96"],"copy|Drugs — noninsulin (Table 99-11, p1577–1578)":["u97"],"copy|Drugs — insulin (Table 99-12, p1579; dosing p1581–1582)":["u98"],"copy|Hypoglycemia":["u99"],"copy|Special populations":["u100"],"copy|Exam traps / book self-contradictions (flagged in source)":["u101"],"copy|6.":["u102"],"copy|What are the approximate prevalences of diabetes and of prediabetes among US adults 65 and older?":["u103"],"copy|~27% diabetes, ~50% prediabetes (p1562).":["u104"],"copy|7.":["u105"],"copy|What eGFR and albuminuria values define diabetic kidney disease?":["u106"],"copy|Usually eGFR <60 mL/min/1.73m² and/or albuminuria >30 mg/g creatinine (p1562).":["u107"],"copy|9.":["u108"],"copy|Which guideline provides the \"Good/Intermediate/Poor health\" framework for individualizing glycemic targets in older adults, and which US standards mirror it?":["u109"],"copy|The 2019 Endocrine Society guideline (Table 99-6); a similar framework appears in the ADA 2021 Standards of Care (p1570).":["u110"],"copy|13.":["u111"],"copy|Which sulfonylurea should be avoided in older adults, and why?":["u112"],"copy|Glyburide — long half-life raises hypoglycemia risk (p1579).":["u113"],"copy|These are the actual 2026 specialist-exam questions and keys sourced to this chapter, reproduced verbatim (English rendering; Hebrew original is the authority) as printed in the chapter's own \"2026 exam\" section.":["u114"],"copy|Q6 — sourced to p.":["u115"],"copy|1574.":["u116"],"copy|A 72-year-old man diagnosed with type 2 diabetes comes to the clinic for lifestyle counselling.":["u117"],"copy|The patient is interested in starting a physical-activity program as recommended by the American Diabetes Association (ADA).":["u118"],"copy|Which of the following guidelines is recommended for him?":["u119"],"copy|Book: p1574: \"The ADA recommends that all adults with diabetes should perform at least 150 min/week of moderate-intensity aerobic PA, spread over at least 3 days/week with no more than 2 consecutive days without exercise.\"":["u120"],"copy|Q25 — sourced to p. 1568 and 1571.":["u121"],"copy|A 71-year-old woman, independent in function and mobile, followed at a diabetes clinic for 4 years, comes to the clinic for a visit.":["u122"],"copy|HBA1C = 7.4%; no known hypoglycemic events.":["u123"],"copy|She receives: Insulin Toujeo 16 units X 1; T.Jardiance 10 mg x 1; T.Glucomin 850 mg x 1.":["u124"],"copy|What is important to recommend at the clinic visit?":["u125"],"copy|Book: p1568: \"both the ADA and Endocrine Society Guidelines recommend that older patients with diabetes should have periodic screening for cognitive impairment: at the time of diagnosis, then annually for those with borderline results initially or every 2 to 3 years when initial screening is normal.\"":["u126"],"copy|Table 99-6 (p1571): Group 1 (good health), on a drug that may cause hypoglycemia (insulin): ≥7.0 and <7.5% — 7.4% is inside that range.":["u127"],"copy|Q37 — sourced to p.":["u128"],"copy|1571.":["u129"],"copy|An 85-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes for 15 years lives in a nursing-care facility.":["u130"],"copy|Background: coronary heart disease, heart failure, stage 3 chronic kidney disease, and moderate dementia.":["u131"],"copy|She needs help with activities of daily living.":["u132"],"copy|Current HbA1c 8.1% on JARDIANCE.":["u133"],"copy|What is the appropriate treatment for her?":["u134"],"copy|Book: Table 99-6 (p1571): Group 3 (poor health) is \"Any one of the following\" (the book sets \"one\" in bold): end-stage medical condition(s), moderate to severe dementia, ≥2 ADL impairments, residence in a long-term nursing facility.":["u135"],"copy|She meets three of these separately: nursing-facility residence, moderate dementia, and dependence in daily activities.":["u136"],"copy|The table's drug rows name the drugs that may cause hypoglycemia as insulin, sulfonylurea and glinides; empagliflozin (JARDIANCE) is none of these, and Table 99-11 (p1578) lists SGLT-2 inhibitors with \"Minimal hypoglycemia\".":["u137"],"copy|So she is in the \"No\" row, where the Group 3 target is <8.5% — 8.1% is inside it.":["u138"],"copy|Q49 — sourced to p.":["u139"],"copy|1563.":["u140"],"copy|Which metabolic change is related to aging in people without diabetes?":["u141"],"copy|Book: p1563: \"In normal people who do not meet criteria for either diabetes mellitus or IGT, there is a slight age-related increase in FPGs and a more dramatic slowing of return to normal of glucose levels following an oral glucose challenge.\"":["u142"],"copy|(This also rules out option 1: fasting glucose rises slightly.)":["u143"],"copy|Same page, next section: \"There is a decline in sensitivity to the metabolic effects of insulin with age.\" (rules out option 3).":["u144"],"copy|Option 2, blood insulin level, is not addressed on p1563.":["u145"],"copy|Q59 — sourced to p.":["u146"],"copy|1577.":["u147"],"copy|A 76-year-old woman, independent and active, with type 2 diabetes for 15 years, hypertension and ischemic heart disease.":["u148"],"copy|Laboratory tests: eGFR = 18 mL/min/1.73m²; Albumin/Creatinine = 450 mg/gr; HbA1C = 7%; Glucose = 110 mg/dL.":["u149"],"copy|She is treated with Metformin at a dose of 2000 mg a day and a DPP-4 inhibitor.":["u150"],"copy|What is the recommendation regarding drug treatment for diabetes in this case?":["u151"],"copy|Book: Table 99-11 (p1577), biguanide: \"Contraindicated if eGFR < 30 mL/min/1.73 m²\"; p1576: \"Metformin is contraindicated in patients with eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m².\"":["u152"],"copy|Table 99-11 (p1578), SGLT-2 inhibitors: \"Renal dose adjustment and avoid if eGFR < 25–30 mL/min/1.73 m²\".":["u153"],"copy|Sulfonylureas (p1577): hypoglycemia, esp. with longer half-life: glyburide.":["u154"],"copy|Answer":["u155","u156","u157","u158"],"copy|CVD-outcome-proven drug classes (large CVOTs, average participant age >60): SGLT-2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, canagliflozin, dapagliflozin) and GLP-1 agonists (liraglutide, semaglutide, dulaglutide) (p1570–1571).":["u40"],"what-the-exams-asked|What the exams asked":["u159"],"copy|Selecting fasting glucose, blood pressure and A1C targets by function and medication risk — 2020 Q62, December 2021 Q93, June 2022 Q28, June 2026 Q25, June 2026 Q37, June 2025 Q73.":["u160"],"copy|Classifying an A1C of 6.2% and deciding whom to screen after age 65 — December 2021 Q32, September 2024 Q92.":["u161"],"copy|Postchallenge glucose, central adiposity and insulin sensitivity with aging — September 2024 Q45, June 2026 Q49.":["u162"],"copy|Lifestyle prevention versus metformin and how to schedule exercise — May 2024 Q1, June 2025 Q44, June 2026 Q6.":["u163"],"copy|Executive dysfunction and when to repeat cognitive screening — December 2021 Q93, September 2024 Q29, June 2026 Q25.":["u164"],"copy|Renal insulin clearance, autonomic counterregulation and poor vision as hypoglycemia risks — December 2021 Q81, June 2025 Q18, June 2023 Basic Q134.":["u165"],"copy|Which diabetes agents protect the heart and kidneys, and when metformin must stop — June 2023 Q68, May 2024 Q91, June 2026 Q59.":["u166"],"copy|Distinguishing SGLT2 adverse effects from other drug toxicities — 2020 Q58, June 2026 Basic Q147.":["u167"],"copy|Statins, blood pressure, triglycerides and the role of aspirin — May 2024 Q6.":["u168"],"copy|Evaluating albuminuria and recognizing cranial or autonomic diabetic neuropathy — September 2024 Q71, June 2026 Basic Q126, June 2025 Basic Q107.":["u169"],"copy|Relieving symptomatic hyperglycemia and replacing low-dose mealtime insulin with an oral agent (ADA 2022) — June 2023 Q24, June 2023 Q70.":["u170"],"copy|Recognizing hyperosmolar dehydration and distinguishing it from ketoacid production — June 2026 Basic Q104, June 2023 Basic Q114.":["u171"],"copy|Liberalizing the diet in hospice when prognosis is measured in months — June 2025 Q85.":["u172"],"choosing-a-goal-for-the-person-in-front-of-you|Choosing a goal for the person in front of you":["u173"],"copy|row:Overall health|TH|Overall health":["u174"],"copy|row:Overall health|TH|Without insulin, sulfonylurea or glinide":["u175"],"copy|row:Overall health|TH|With one of these hypoglycemia-prone drugs":["u176"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Good":["u177"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|A1C below 7.5%, fasting 90–130, bedtime 90–150":["u178"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|A1C 7.0% to below 7.5%, fasting 90–150, bedtime 100–180":["u179"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Intermediate":["u180"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|A1C below 8.0%, fasting 90–150, bedtime 100–180":["u181"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|A1C 7.5% to below 8.0%, fasting 100–150, bedtime 150–180":["u182"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Poor":["u183"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|A1C below 8.5%, fasting 100–180, bedtime 110–200":["u184"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|A1C 8.0% to below 8.5%, fasting 100–180, bedtime 150–250":["u185"],"diagnosing-dysglycemia-and-deciding-whom-to-screen|Diagnosing dysglycemia and deciding whom to screen":["u186"],"aging-prevention-and-an-exercise-prescription|Aging, prevention and an exercise prescription":["u187"],"cognition-self-management-and-the-reason-for-a-low|Cognition, self-management and the reason for a low":["u188"],"kidney-disease-drug-selection-and-cardiovascular-outcomes|Kidney disease, drug selection and cardiovascular outcomes":["u189"],"complications-at-presentation-and-during-surveillance|Complications at presentation and during surveillance":["u190"],"insulin-hyperglycemic-crises-and-care-near-the-end-of-life|Insulin, hyperglycemic crises and care near the end of life":["u191"],"nutrition-education-and-follow-up|Nutrition, education and follow-up":["u192"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u193"],"copy|The 2020–2022 labels open the chapter head because those sittings have no 8e page mapping.":["u194"],"copy|June 2023 Q24 and Q70 cite ADA 2022.":["u195"],"copy|Their Read links lead to the related insulin passages, and the guideline locators appear in the insulin section below.":["u196"],"copy|Classify health before choosing a number.":["u197"],"copy|In Hazzard's 2019 Endocrine Society framework, good health means no more than two non-diabetes chronic illnesses, no basic ADL impairment and at most one IADL impairment.":["u198"],"copy|Three or more chronic illnesses, early cognitive impairment or at least two IADL impairments suggest intermediate health.":["u199"],"copy|An end-stage condition, moderate-to-severe dementia, dependence in at least two basic ADLs or long-term nursing-facility residence each qualifies as poor health.":["u200"],"copy|Figure 99-7 shows why birthday alone is insufficient.":["u201"],"copy|More than half of those over 75 were relatively healthy by the study's criteria (pp1569–1571).":["u202"],"copy|Glucose ranges are in mg/dL.":["u203"],"copy|The lower A1C boundary in the right column reflects concern about hypoglycemia and mortality, especially with insulin.":["u204"],"copy|Ask about actual lows even when the average looks satisfactory.":["u205"],"copy|An A1C near 8.5% corresponds to an average glucose around 200 mg/dL.":["u206"],"copy|Further relaxation can permit glycosuria, dehydration and impaired wound healing.":["u207"],"copy|Shared goals can move in either direction with symptoms, safety and preferences (p1571, Table 99-6).":["u208"],"copy|Apply the rows.":["u209"],"copy|The independent insulin-treated woman in June 2026 Q25 had an A1C of 7.4% without reported lows.":["u210"],"copy|Periodic cognitive assessment is more informative than adding mealtime insulin.":["u211"],"copy|The nursing-facility resident taking empagliflozin in Q37 had moderate dementia, needed ADL help and had an A1C of 8.1%.":["u212"],"copy|Continuing tolerated therapy fits her priorities.":["u213"],"copy|June 2025 Q73 asked for the book's poor-health A1C ceiling without insulin.":["u214"],"copy|The older ADA 2020 questions used their own Table 12.1.":["u215"],"copy|It lists fasting 90–130, A1C below 7.5% and BP below 140/90 in the healthy group.":["u216"],"copy|The fasting ceiling of 150 and BP 140/80 in the credited 2020 Q62 choice do not match that row.":["u217"],"copy|In advanced dementia, the ADA 2020 row used fasting 100–180 and A1C below 8.5%.":["u218"],"copy|ADA 2020, Table 12.1":["u219"],"copy|Current clinical distinction.":["u220"],"copy|ADA 2026 advises avoiding reliance on A1C in very complex or poor health.":["u221"],"copy|Use glucose patterns to prevent hypoglycemia and symptomatic hyperglycemia.":["u222"],"copy|Its treated BP goal is below 130/80 for most older adults when safely attainable, with a more relaxed target such as below 140/90 for poor health, limited life expectancy or substantial treatment risk.":["u223"],"copy|ADA 2026, Table 13.2 and recommendation 13.9":["u224"],"copy|The three diagnostic tests sample different physiology.":["u225"],"copy|Fasting glucose reflects basal glucose regulation, a two-hour value tests recovery after a 75-g load, and A1C averages exposure over preceding weeks.":["u226"],"copy|Any one of a confirmed fasting plasma glucose of at least 126 mg/dL after eight hours without calories, a two-hour glucose of at least 200 mg/dL or an A1C of at least 6.5% meets a diabetes criterion.":["u227"],"copy|Classic symptoms or hyperglycemic crisis with a random glucose of at least 200 mg/dL provide another route.":["u228"],"copy|Prediabetes corresponds to fasting 100–125, two-hour 140–199, or A1C 5.7%–6.4% (pp1559–1561, Table 99-1).":["u229"],"copy|When tests disagree.":["u230"],"copy|Repeat the abnormal test in an asymptomatic patient.":["u231"],"copy|Isolated postchallenge hyperglycemia is relatively frequent in older people and predicts complications even with a fasting glucose below 126.":["u2