ng doses, planning meals or responding to alarms even when conversational ability seems preserved.":["u267"],"copy|Ask the patient to demonstrate monitoring and dosing.":["u268"],"copy|Screen at diagnosis.":["u269"],"copy|The chapter suggests yearly repetition after a borderline screen and every two to three years after a normal screen.":["u270"],"copy|A positive screen merits diagnostic assessment.":["u271"],"copy|The dated ADA 2020 recommendation used in December 2021 Q93 calls for initial assessment from age 65 and annual screening as appropriate.":["u272"],"copy|Diabetes is associated with both vascular dementia and Alzheimer disease.":["u273"],"copy|Cognitive impairment can produce poor control and severe lows, and hypoglycemia is itself associated with dementia (p1568 and ADA 2020 recommendation 12.3).":["u274"],"copy|Find the failed defense.":["u275"],"copy|Glucagon and the sympathoadrenal response normally counter falling glucose.":["u276"],"copy|Autonomic dysfunction reduces warning, the glucagon and growth-hormone responses can weaken with age, and beta blockade may impair adrenergic defenses.":["u277"],"copy|The kidney clears part of an insulin dose, so falling filtration can prolong exposure.":["u278"],"copy|Liver failure can limit compensatory hepatic glucose release.":["u279"],"copy|Repeated prior lows can lower the threshold for warning until it approaches neuroglycopenia.":["u280"],"copy|Avoiding further lows can improve awareness.":["u281"],"copy|Check meals, alcohol or sedatives, polypharmacy, cognition, hand strength, hearing, mobility and vision.":["u282"],"copy|Poor vision can cause misreading of glucose or an insulin pen.":["u283"],"copy|These are different explanations for the December 2021, June 2025 and June 2023 Basic questions (pp1583–1584, Tables 99-14 and 99-15).":["u284"],"copy|When the mean is misleading.":["u285"],"copy|A patient can have an A1C above goal and still have dangerous fasting lows.":["u286"],"copy|Review the pattern, food